WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.520
Over the years, I've seen multiple five-digit bounties

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:05.840
awarded for request smuggling bugs.

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:09.400
So naturally, I'd like to find one myself,

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:12.400
but to be honest, I never did anything more

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:15.700
than just trying to get tool like request smuggler.

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:18.760
So to learn how people are actually earning money

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:19.920
with request smuggling,

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:22.719
I extracted all the reports from the internet

00:00:22.719 --> 00:00:24.360
and I studied them.

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:26.000
In this video, I will show you

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:29.760
what is the most common root cause of request smuggling

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:33.759
and also what are the potential areas for future research.

00:00:33.759 --> 00:00:34.599
Enjoy.

00:00:35.639 --> 00:00:38.080
The most common category that I've noticed

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:41.160
was white space characters in content length

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:43.320
or transfer encoding headers.

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:48.320
So we can see big bounties awarded in this category.

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:50.400
For example, this one in Apple,

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:54.040
a quick recap of what request smuggling even is.

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:57.360
You have a few different ways to tell the server

00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:59.840
what is the length of your request.

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:02.959
The most common that we see when we send requests

00:01:02.959 --> 00:01:05.160
from the browser is the content length header,

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:08.639
which just tells the server how long our request is.

00:01:08.639 --> 00:01:11.559
But there's also transfer encoding chunk header,

00:01:11.559 --> 00:01:16.160
which sort of tells the length of the next chunk

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:19.360
and then until it meets zero,

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:22.120
it means there are more chunks to be sent.

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:23.199
And the thing is,

00:01:23.199 --> 00:01:26.540
even though we don't see it sent from the browsers,

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:30.739
usually this has the priority over the content length header

00:01:30.739 --> 00:01:35.059
and most of request smuggling bugs occur

00:01:35.059 --> 00:01:36.900
because one of the component,

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:38.779
either the front end or the back end,

00:01:38.779 --> 00:01:41.320
incorrectly interprets these requests

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:44.059
and either prioritizes them incorrectly

00:01:44.059 --> 00:01:46.620
or doesn't see one of these headers.

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:51.620
So this category white space characters in CL or TE headers,

00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:55.580
it means to obstruct one of these headers,

00:01:55.580 --> 00:01:58.419
usually transfer encoding from one of the components.

00:01:58.419 --> 00:02:00.099
And I've seen everything.

00:02:00.099 --> 00:02:03.459
I've seen spaces starts before the header,

00:02:03.459 --> 00:02:07.580
before the colon, after the colon, after the value,

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:09.059
just everything.

00:02:09.059 --> 00:02:10.020
In this case,

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:14.500
we have the new line just after the header name,

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:15.660
transfer encoding.

00:02:16.619 --> 00:02:19.740
It actually, and then we have the space here,

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:20.860
which is quite important

00:02:20.899 --> 00:02:25.899
because it looks like a invalid HTTP request.

00:02:26.619 --> 00:02:30.059
Like there's a header name with no column here

00:02:30.059 --> 00:02:32.059
and here the line starts with a space.

00:02:32.059 --> 00:02:34.580
It looks like an invalid line,

00:02:34.580 --> 00:02:37.860
but it actually is a multi-line header

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:42.860
because when the header line starts with a space,

00:02:42.979 --> 00:02:45.899
it means that what follows

00:02:45.899 --> 00:02:49.259
is the continuation of the previous header.

00:02:49.259 --> 00:02:51.979
I don't know if the specification allows the colon

00:02:51.979 --> 00:02:53.539
to be in the new line,

00:02:53.539 --> 00:02:55.139
but if you would have, for example,

00:02:55.139 --> 00:02:57.979
transfer encoding colon chunked,

00:02:57.979 --> 00:03:02.179
and then in the next line you would have chunked again,

00:03:02.179 --> 00:03:05.500
it would be a valid continuation of the value.

00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:08.380
So this bug makes a lot of sense

00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:10.419
and just one of the components

00:03:10.419 --> 00:03:13.860
bit treated this differently than the other component,

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:17.419
which led to request smuggling here in Apple,

00:03:17.419 --> 00:03:22.419
for which there was $36,000 of reward.

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:24.979
So quite a great bug.

00:03:24.979 --> 00:03:26.020
And as I told you,

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:28.619
I've seen many different variations

00:03:28.619 --> 00:03:31.899
of different new line characters, tabs, spaces,

00:03:31.899 --> 00:03:36.899
0x09, 0x07, null bytes inside header.

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:39.940
There was just all the different variations.

00:03:39.940 --> 00:03:44.940
There was a total of 26 requests like this.

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:46.220
Then the second group

00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:51.220
is just incorrect prioritization of CL and TE headers.

00:03:53.380 --> 00:03:54.660
And you could actually argue

00:03:54.660 --> 00:03:57.300
that every single of these reports

00:03:57.300 --> 00:03:58.820
or almost all of these reports

00:03:58.820 --> 00:04:02.740
are incorrect prioritization of these headers.

00:04:02.740 --> 00:04:04.940
That's such a hard word.

00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:08.380
But in most of these reports like here,

00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:12.619
the priorities were straight in all components.

00:04:12.619 --> 00:04:14.020
I assume all components

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:17.579
would prioritize transfer encoding

00:04:17.579 --> 00:04:19.540
over the content length header.

00:04:19.540 --> 00:04:23.619
Just one component did not see this as a valid header.

00:04:23.619 --> 00:04:26.220
And in these 13 reports that I have here,

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:30.619
there was just invalid order

00:04:30.619 --> 00:04:32.739
just when there was both transfer encoding

00:04:32.739 --> 00:04:34.260
and content length header,

00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:36.859
the content length would get the priority,

00:04:36.859 --> 00:04:39.980
which is not what the specification says.

00:04:39.980 --> 00:04:40.820
So it is bad.

00:04:40.820 --> 00:04:45.820
A lot of these reports are from the sort of some time ago

00:04:45.899 --> 00:04:50.899
for like here, 2019, this is where the original article,

00:04:51.140 --> 00:04:51.980
well, not the original,

00:04:51.980 --> 00:04:54.299
the article by James Kettle

00:04:54.299 --> 00:04:58.940
about HTTP desync being reborn was published.

00:04:58.940 --> 00:05:02.660
And some of these reports

00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:05.100
where you just send both headers,

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:08.980
the server prioritizes the incorrect one

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:10.420
and that's the bug.

00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:14.339
And most newer request smuggling bugs

00:05:14.339 --> 00:05:17.100
that I've seen required something more

00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:20.019
like required either the obfuscation

00:05:20.019 --> 00:05:22.500
by those white space characters

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:26.700
that I've shown you before or something else.

00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:27.980
We'll go to the next group,

00:05:27.980 --> 00:05:30.059
which is multiple transfer encoding

00:05:30.059 --> 00:05:34.779
or CTLs or transfer encoding or content length headers.

00:05:35.779 --> 00:05:38.980
Here, for example, we have a request like this

00:05:38.980 --> 00:05:41.420
with one content length

00:05:41.420 --> 00:05:43.739
and two transfer encoding headers.

00:05:43.739 --> 00:05:47.660
Just one of the transfer encoding is invalid.

00:05:47.660 --> 00:05:49.579
So I assume what happens is

00:05:49.579 --> 00:05:52.820
one component only takes the last value

00:05:52.820 --> 00:05:55.859
of transfer encoding sees that it is invalid.

00:05:55.859 --> 00:06:00.299
So it instead decides to use the content length header

00:06:00.299 --> 00:06:02.820
and the other component sees

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:05.579
either just takes the first value of the header

00:06:05.579 --> 00:06:08.619
when there are multiple headers with the same name

00:06:08.619 --> 00:06:11.420
or maybe combine these values together.

00:06:11.420 --> 00:06:12.739
But one way or another,

00:06:12.739 --> 00:06:15.700
it does see the transfer encoding header

00:06:15.700 --> 00:06:18.500
and the component that sees

00:06:18.500 --> 00:06:22.859
that the chunk transfer encoding sees the request end here

00:06:22.859 --> 00:06:25.459
and sees this being the next request.

00:06:25.459 --> 00:06:27.380
And then this victims request,

00:06:27.380 --> 00:06:31.179
which can be a random request from another user

00:06:31.179 --> 00:06:35.859
is like the continuation of this request

00:06:36.739 --> 00:06:41.660
while the other component sees this header,

00:06:41.660 --> 00:06:44.739
I assume 69 ends somewhere here

00:06:44.739 --> 00:06:49.739
and just sees this as the next header as it expects.

00:06:50.179 --> 00:06:52.100
So this is what caused this bug.

00:06:52.100 --> 00:06:53.940
We only had seven of these,

00:06:54.980 --> 00:06:57.540
but that's what caused them.

00:06:58.420 --> 00:07:01.140
And before you ask me if I can share all these reports,

00:07:01.980 --> 00:07:05.140
I am always sharing these reports in BBRE Premium Archive.

00:07:05.140 --> 00:07:09.140
The article is generally for BBRE Premium subscribers

00:07:09.140 --> 00:07:10.339
for the members.

00:07:10.339 --> 00:07:12.579
And the free video is usually based

00:07:12.579 --> 00:07:14.859
on about one third of the case study

00:07:14.859 --> 00:07:17.820
and the database is also in the paid version.

00:07:17.820 --> 00:07:18.980
And that's where you can find it

00:07:18.980 --> 00:07:21.500
is the first link in the description.

00:07:22.420 --> 00:07:25.260
The next category that I have is ignoring

00:07:25.260 --> 00:07:28.260
the TE or CL headers.

00:07:29.260 --> 00:07:31.579
And we can see from seven reports

00:07:31.579 --> 00:07:33.100
that I have in this category,

00:07:33.100 --> 00:07:36.859
four are from James Kell's article.

00:07:36.859 --> 00:07:40.940
The thing is I try to extract separate reports

00:07:40.940 --> 00:07:43.940
from his articles to sort of get,

00:07:43.940 --> 00:07:46.059
especially when the exploitation

00:07:46.059 --> 00:07:47.619
was actually a bit different,

00:07:47.619 --> 00:07:49.459
but often he wouldn't mention the bounty.

00:07:49.459 --> 00:07:51.779
So I don't know the bounties for this.

00:07:52.859 --> 00:07:57.140
I only have one bug with the bounty in this category.

00:07:57.220 --> 00:08:01.100
What does it mean to ignore the content length header?

00:08:01.100 --> 00:08:04.140
The thing is it's the newer research,

00:08:04.140 --> 00:08:07.140
the browser powered desync attacks.

00:08:07.140 --> 00:08:10.739
So some endpoints would just ignore

00:08:10.739 --> 00:08:14.980
the content length header, even if you send it.

00:08:14.980 --> 00:08:17.579
So it doesn't matter if the content length is zero

00:08:17.579 --> 00:08:20.420
or if it has an actual value with it.

00:08:21.459 --> 00:08:25.260
The server would just not look at this value.

00:08:26.140 --> 00:08:30.859
So here we have the case where we use

00:08:30.859 --> 00:08:34.419
the content length of 311 in this case.

00:08:34.419 --> 00:08:37.140
So this whole second request should be

00:08:37.140 --> 00:08:40.059
the body of the first request,

00:08:40.059 --> 00:08:42.460
but the server, because it just redirect,

00:08:42.460 --> 00:08:44.539
it doesn't actually need the body,

00:08:44.539 --> 00:08:46.260
but it should still consume it.

00:08:47.900 --> 00:08:49.099
But it ignores it.

00:08:49.099 --> 00:08:51.619
And that's why it just sees two different requests

00:08:51.619 --> 00:08:54.460
and issues two different responses.

00:08:54.500 --> 00:08:58.419
And the great thing about this variation

00:08:58.419 --> 00:09:01.099
of request smuggling or desync

00:09:01.099 --> 00:09:06.059
is that you can send these two perfectly valid requests

00:09:06.059 --> 00:09:07.700
or to be more precise,

00:09:07.700 --> 00:09:11.460
this one big request with this as a body.

00:09:11.460 --> 00:09:13.739
You can send this from the browser,

00:09:13.739 --> 00:09:16.580
which allows client-side attacks,

00:09:16.580 --> 00:09:21.580
which allows to attack the victim inside the browser.

00:09:21.739 --> 00:09:23.619
And this is a great bug.

00:09:24.820 --> 00:09:29.780
And I think usually you're able to achieve the XSS with this

00:09:29.780 --> 00:09:33.460
because you can, as you can see,

00:09:33.460 --> 00:09:35.219
you can just, in this case,

00:09:36.299 --> 00:09:40.260
you can just issue a request to attackers URL,

00:09:40.260 --> 00:09:42.739
because we can see that we have Azure victim

00:09:42.739 --> 00:09:43.900
and Azure attacker.

00:09:43.900 --> 00:09:47.059
So a different host serving the XSS,

00:09:47.059 --> 00:09:49.219
but the browser doesn't know it's a different host.

00:09:49.219 --> 00:09:53.219
The browser just thinks it's a one request

00:09:53.219 --> 00:09:55.900
with the response,

00:09:55.900 --> 00:09:58.179
one response where these are the headers

00:09:58.179 --> 00:09:59.859
and this is the body.

00:09:59.859 --> 00:10:04.859
So a great research and a great, great bugs as well.

00:10:05.580 --> 00:10:10.500
Then for request, we have not closing the connection,

00:10:10.500 --> 00:10:13.700
which I have the example here.

00:10:14.580 --> 00:10:16.659
The thing is when you send something invalid

00:10:16.659 --> 00:10:18.299
and the server denies it,

00:10:18.299 --> 00:10:20.299
it should also close the connection

00:10:20.299 --> 00:10:24.260
to sort of not allow you to send more data.

00:10:24.260 --> 00:10:27.700
So if you send a request that's too large,

00:10:27.700 --> 00:10:29.419
if you send an invalid header,

00:10:29.419 --> 00:10:30.979
if you do things like this

00:10:32.059 --> 00:10:34.619
and the server decides it's a bad request,

00:10:34.619 --> 00:10:36.659
it should close the connection

00:10:36.659 --> 00:10:39.219
because when it doesn't,

00:10:39.219 --> 00:10:42.419
it leads to strange things happening.

00:10:42.419 --> 00:10:45.859
Like here, we actually have this article

00:10:45.859 --> 00:10:48.179
has like five different bugs,

00:10:49.059 --> 00:10:50.820
but this particular one,

00:10:50.820 --> 00:10:54.419
you just send a request that's too large.

00:10:54.419 --> 00:10:58.460
The server replies that it's an invalid HTTP request,

00:10:58.460 --> 00:11:01.059
but it keeps the connection open.

00:11:01.059 --> 00:11:04.380
And what follows,

00:11:04.380 --> 00:11:07.780
so you send the exact value of bytes

00:11:07.780 --> 00:11:10.460
that makes the request too large.

00:11:10.460 --> 00:11:12.500
And straight after that,

00:11:12.500 --> 00:11:17.340
it will consider the next thing, a new request,

00:11:17.859 --> 00:11:20.260
previous request was not finished.

00:11:20.260 --> 00:11:24.460
So you can just sort of continue talking to the server,

00:11:24.460 --> 00:11:28.780
even though you send a request that was too large,

00:11:28.780 --> 00:11:32.299
which, yeah, it led to four bugs,

00:11:32.299 --> 00:11:35.619
these behaviors not closing the connection.

00:11:35.619 --> 00:11:39.140
So it is something to watch out for.

00:11:39.140 --> 00:11:44.140
Then I have as well four bugs in the HTTP2 version.

00:11:45.140 --> 00:11:49.539
Again, three of those come from the article

00:11:49.539 --> 00:11:51.340
by James Kettle.

00:11:51.340 --> 00:11:53.260
And I was also sort of wondering,

00:11:53.260 --> 00:11:56.619
I'm still wondering what's the reason

00:11:56.619 --> 00:11:59.739
that sort of this great research

00:11:59.739 --> 00:12:04.179
does not lead to a lot of other bugs being found,

00:12:04.179 --> 00:12:05.940
or at least other bugs being published

00:12:05.940 --> 00:12:08.219
because maybe people are making a ton of money on this

00:12:08.219 --> 00:12:10.380
and they are not publishing it.

00:12:10.380 --> 00:12:12.500
But yeah, it's strange.

00:12:12.500 --> 00:12:13.619
There's a great research.

00:12:14.099 --> 00:12:14.940
There are great bugs.

00:12:14.940 --> 00:12:16.739
And then there is nothing.

00:12:16.739 --> 00:12:18.979
And I wonder what happens.

00:12:18.979 --> 00:12:22.580
These bugs, this is another article.

00:12:22.580 --> 00:12:25.700
This one is from 21, 2021.

00:12:26.619 --> 00:12:29.739
And what happened here was the downgrading

00:12:29.739 --> 00:12:32.979
from HTTP2 to HTTP1.

00:12:32.979 --> 00:12:37.979
So the server would use HTTP2 when talking with the client,

00:12:38.700 --> 00:12:41.700
but then when the front end would talk with the backend,

00:12:41.739 --> 00:12:45.059
it would use HTTP1 again.

00:12:45.059 --> 00:12:47.619
And normally it's not a problem,

00:12:47.619 --> 00:12:52.179
but it becomes the problem when like here,

00:12:52.179 --> 00:12:55.900
it also forwards a very important header

00:12:55.900 --> 00:12:58.659
like content length without checking

00:12:58.659 --> 00:13:00.739
if it's a valid one or not.

00:13:00.739 --> 00:13:04.299
So you sent, this is a sort of HTTP2 syntax

00:13:04.299 --> 00:13:08.619
because in HTTP2, the content length header

00:13:09.020 --> 00:13:12.780
is not really used because it's a binary protocol

00:13:12.780 --> 00:13:15.539
and it doesn't use this to decide

00:13:15.539 --> 00:13:16.739
where does the request finish.

00:13:16.739 --> 00:13:19.500
So you just send this as a body,

00:13:19.500 --> 00:13:22.460
but then when front end would be talking with the backend,

00:13:23.419 --> 00:13:26.580
with HTTP1, it does need the content length header

00:13:26.580 --> 00:13:29.340
or the transfer encoding chunk, doesn't matter.

00:13:29.340 --> 00:13:31.820
And then it just uses the content length

00:13:31.820 --> 00:13:35.140
sent by the client without verifying it.

00:13:35.140 --> 00:13:38.380
So the backend would think the request ends here

00:13:39.140 --> 00:13:42.299
and this is a new request, which is of course not true.

00:13:42.299 --> 00:13:44.659
And this led to request smuggling.

00:13:44.659 --> 00:13:49.659
We had 20K Netflix, 10K Verizon, 7K Verizon,

00:13:49.940 --> 00:13:53.140
seven and a half K Basecamp and surely more bounties

00:13:53.140 --> 00:13:54.419
just by James Hell

00:13:54.419 --> 00:13:56.099
because he doesn't disclose all the bugs

00:13:56.099 --> 00:13:57.659
that he's discovered.

00:13:57.659 --> 00:14:01.299
And I wonder what other ones are out there.

00:14:02.299 --> 00:14:07.299
Then we have fun ones with Node doing fun stuff.

00:14:08.260 --> 00:14:13.260
I have three reports in the only using new line

00:14:13.460 --> 00:14:18.460
or carriage return characters as a line terminator.

00:14:18.900 --> 00:14:23.500
There were a few fun reports with Node doing strange stuff.

00:14:24.619 --> 00:14:27.140
In this case, Node decided...

00:14:27.140 --> 00:14:32.140
So in generally, HTTP1 relies heavily on text parsing

00:14:32.659 --> 00:14:36.419
and on new lines because it separates different headers

00:14:36.460 --> 00:14:38.580
in the body and the end of the request

00:14:38.580 --> 00:14:41.059
using where the new lines are.

00:14:41.059 --> 00:14:42.979
And the new line is actually two characters,

00:14:42.979 --> 00:14:45.739
usually carriage return and line feed.

00:14:46.619 --> 00:14:49.500
So, and this is very important,

00:14:49.500 --> 00:14:51.940
but Node for some reason decided it's enough

00:14:51.940 --> 00:14:53.780
if you just have the carriage return,

00:14:53.780 --> 00:14:58.780
which is usually denoted as backslash R or percent zero D.

00:15:00.380 --> 00:15:05.380
And here, no other server would consider this

00:15:05.940 --> 00:15:09.979
being a new line, but Node did.

00:15:09.979 --> 00:15:14.979
And it did consider this to be a valid

00:15:15.539 --> 00:15:17.659
transfer encoding chunked header,

00:15:17.659 --> 00:15:20.979
which of course is exactly what you need

00:15:20.979 --> 00:15:23.659
to desync the backend with the frontend.

00:15:24.580 --> 00:15:27.380
And these were reported $1,800

00:15:27.380 --> 00:15:29.099
from the internet bug bounty.

00:15:30.179 --> 00:15:32.820
The other one is quite similar as well,

00:15:32.820 --> 00:15:34.380
but we'll go to the next category

00:15:34.380 --> 00:15:36.979
where the server would treat

00:15:36.979 --> 00:15:40.179
not a literal chunked transfer encoding

00:15:40.179 --> 00:15:41.739
as chunk transfer encoding.

00:15:42.780 --> 00:15:45.619
For example, this is a fun one.

00:15:45.619 --> 00:15:47.299
I did publish a video.

00:15:47.299 --> 00:15:48.979
I will link it in the description

00:15:48.979 --> 00:15:52.219
about this request smuggling back with the AJP protocol.

00:15:52.219 --> 00:15:54.659
It was a CTF task with the CVE.

00:15:55.500 --> 00:16:00.500
And the thing was the code was just only looking

00:16:00.539 --> 00:16:04.619
specifically for the chunked transfer encoding chunk

00:16:04.619 --> 00:16:06.780
and nothing else would work.

00:16:06.780 --> 00:16:09.460
But the other component or the other piece of code

00:16:09.460 --> 00:16:13.539
of the same component would treat a value

00:16:13.539 --> 00:16:15.859
like transfer encoding chunked chunked

00:16:17.140 --> 00:16:18.979
or chunked comma chunked

00:16:18.979 --> 00:16:21.580
as a valid transfer encoding chunked header,

00:16:21.580 --> 00:16:25.979
which was also enough to desync the server.

00:16:25.979 --> 00:16:28.859
And it was a fun exploiting this one.

00:16:28.900 --> 00:16:31.219
It was quite hard because the AJP protocol

00:16:31.219 --> 00:16:33.700
is something you probably don't know about,

00:16:34.700 --> 00:16:37.099
but it was very, very fun.

00:16:38.059 --> 00:16:40.739
The next group, CRLF injection,

00:16:40.739 --> 00:16:43.419
also three bugs in this category.

00:16:43.419 --> 00:16:48.419
And here it's something a bit different

00:16:48.900 --> 00:16:53.900
because it's not like most of the other reports

00:16:54.700 --> 00:16:56.659
where you sort of send the transfer encoding

00:16:56.659 --> 00:16:57.780
and content length header

00:16:57.780 --> 00:17:01.500
and one component sees different things than another.

00:17:01.500 --> 00:17:04.339
In this case, you just send one request,

00:17:04.339 --> 00:17:07.500
but the front end when forwarding the request

00:17:08.500 --> 00:17:12.219
messes up with the decoding of this request

00:17:12.219 --> 00:17:15.739
and actually makes it like it's two different requests,

00:17:15.739 --> 00:17:18.780
which leads to request smuggling as well.

00:17:18.780 --> 00:17:21.060
I'm actually surprised this is not more common,

00:17:21.060 --> 00:17:25.819
especially I published an article about a great talk

00:17:25.819 --> 00:17:28.060
about this bug and I'm quite surprised

00:17:28.060 --> 00:17:33.060
how easy it is to misconfigure Nginx to make this possible.

00:17:33.420 --> 00:17:36.099
And I'm quite surprised this is not more common

00:17:36.099 --> 00:17:40.579
because of this, but I only saw three reports of this.

00:17:40.579 --> 00:17:44.699
This particular one was rewarded 12 and a half thousand

00:17:44.699 --> 00:17:45.540
dollars.

00:17:46.500 --> 00:17:50.739
Now we go into something that I think

00:17:50.739 --> 00:17:52.859
is a great area for research.

00:17:52.859 --> 00:17:54.380
And it's something I didn't know about

00:17:54.380 --> 00:17:57.060
before doing this case study.

00:17:58.140 --> 00:18:00.739
There is a thing called trailer,

00:18:00.739 --> 00:18:03.420
which means when you have a chunked encoding,

00:18:03.420 --> 00:18:08.219
so it's this, so you have the one chunk of length five here,

00:18:08.219 --> 00:18:10.540
the other chunk of length two here.

00:18:10.540 --> 00:18:13.420
Don't be mistaken, there's no space here in this,

00:18:13.420 --> 00:18:15.859
like in here, in the beginning,

00:18:15.859 --> 00:18:18.300
I thought it's the leading space type of bug,

00:18:18.300 --> 00:18:23.020
but it looks like this because of the line number here,

00:18:24.020 --> 00:18:26.300
which I think shouldn't be like this,

00:18:26.300 --> 00:18:30.180
but it is not the case, there's no space here.

00:18:30.180 --> 00:18:34.219
And the trailer of HTTP request just means

00:18:34.219 --> 00:18:37.300
when you have the chunked body encoding,

00:18:37.300 --> 00:18:42.300
you can still have some headers after or trailer headers.

00:18:42.380 --> 00:18:44.500
I'm not sure if they are treated exactly like

00:18:44.500 --> 00:18:47.060
normal headers, I think they shouldn't.

00:18:47.060 --> 00:18:50.859
So I did, of course, I've never seen this being used

00:18:50.900 --> 00:18:53.300
by an actual application.

00:18:53.300 --> 00:18:56.660
And I also didn't see this in any other bug

00:18:56.660 --> 00:18:57.979
apart from these two ones.

00:18:57.979 --> 00:19:02.979
Both these reports were $4,600, it's a bug in Apache.

00:19:05.660 --> 00:19:10.660
And I'm 100% sure this is such a niche feature of HTTP

00:19:12.020 --> 00:19:14.300
that it is a good area for research

00:19:14.300 --> 00:19:18.579
because there is no way that all the other servers

00:19:18.579 --> 00:19:23.579
consume this trailer correctly because of how rare it is.

00:19:27.180 --> 00:19:32.180
Then the next category is H2C upgrade.

00:19:32.459 --> 00:19:37.459
I've only saw two reports of this in one blog

00:19:37.500 --> 00:19:41.219
posted by AssetNode, which is always a great thing.

00:19:41.219 --> 00:19:44.540
I also saw this in a CTF task and it looked,

00:19:44.540 --> 00:19:47.180
it's not that hard to misconfigure your server

00:19:47.500 --> 00:19:49.420
to be able to do this.

00:19:49.420 --> 00:19:53.739
But we didn't see more of this in the wild.

00:19:53.739 --> 00:19:58.739
The thing is, you can do the upgrade of the HTTP one

00:20:00.099 --> 00:20:04.859
to HTTP two, the way that you bypass the reverse proxy.

00:20:04.859 --> 00:20:08.900
And then, of course, you can bypass the WAF,

00:20:08.900 --> 00:20:10.619
you can bypass the access control

00:20:10.619 --> 00:20:14.060
that's on the sort of middle layer, things like this.

00:20:14.939 --> 00:20:17.020
So it's a fun bug.

00:20:17.020 --> 00:20:18.140
I've tried it a few times.

00:20:18.140 --> 00:20:23.140
There is a tool H2C smuggler on GitHub, which does this.

00:20:25.859 --> 00:20:27.939
Unfortunately, I haven't found this bug,

00:20:27.939 --> 00:20:30.780
but it looks like something that's not,

00:20:30.780 --> 00:20:33.739
the attack scenario is quite reasonable.

00:20:33.739 --> 00:20:37.780
So I'm sure it's always worth checking for this

00:20:37.780 --> 00:20:40.619
when you're testing a target and when this is useful.

00:20:41.619 --> 00:20:44.020
And then we have two last categories

00:20:44.020 --> 00:20:46.339
with only one bug each.

00:20:46.339 --> 00:20:50.380
Here's another fun one with Node doing strange stuff.

00:20:50.380 --> 00:20:55.380
Namely, it would replace the carriage return.

00:20:55.859 --> 00:20:59.339
So as I said, a new line is usually

00:20:59.339 --> 00:21:02.280
carriage return line feed characters.

00:21:02.280 --> 00:21:05.459
And for some reason, Node would replace the carriage return

00:21:05.459 --> 00:21:10.060
with the dash, which seems weird to me.

00:21:10.099 --> 00:21:11.699
I don't know why they did this,

00:21:12.540 --> 00:21:17.260
but this is crucial because the content length header

00:21:17.260 --> 00:21:20.300
normally contains the dash between the word content

00:21:20.300 --> 00:21:21.660
and the word length.

00:21:21.660 --> 00:21:23.579
So we should send a request like this.

00:21:24.459 --> 00:21:26.900
I think it actually should be denied

00:21:26.900 --> 00:21:28.939
because I don't think this character

00:21:28.939 --> 00:21:32.300
is allowed in the header name, but it doesn't matter.

00:21:32.300 --> 00:21:34.219
But one way or another,

00:21:34.219 --> 00:21:36.500
a component that would see this and accept this

00:21:36.500 --> 00:21:38.579
would definitely not consider this

00:21:38.579 --> 00:21:41.140
being a valid content length header,

00:21:41.140 --> 00:21:46.140
but then Node would sort of decode the CR to a hyphen,

00:21:46.459 --> 00:21:48.900
which would make this a valid content length header.

00:21:48.900 --> 00:21:53.500
So the front end would see these as two different requests.

00:21:53.500 --> 00:21:56.660
The back end would see this as one request

00:21:56.660 --> 00:22:01.660
with the body length of 42, which I assume is up to here.

00:22:01.900 --> 00:22:04.819
So this would be a new request.

00:22:04.819 --> 00:22:06.260
So quite a fun one.

00:22:06.300 --> 00:22:08.859
I don't know why Node does this stuff,

00:22:08.859 --> 00:22:11.979
but Node was definitely something that was fun

00:22:11.979 --> 00:22:14.780
during this case study to see the bug like this.

00:22:14.780 --> 00:22:19.300
It was only rewarded $500 by the internet bug bounty,

00:22:19.300 --> 00:22:21.540
but it's still a fun bug.

00:22:21.540 --> 00:22:22.500
And the last category,

00:22:22.500 --> 00:22:26.579
which I also think is a good area for some research

00:22:26.579 --> 00:22:29.219
is chunk extensions,

00:22:29.219 --> 00:22:33.180
which I also up to recently, I didn't know about.

00:22:33.180 --> 00:22:36.060
I just learned, I think it was the motivation for me

00:22:36.859 --> 00:22:37.699
to do this case study.

00:22:37.699 --> 00:22:39.380
I saw a talk about these bugs,

00:22:39.380 --> 00:22:44.380
the HTTP garden tool for testing different HTTP servers.

00:22:46.060 --> 00:22:48.339
And I learned what a chunk extension is.

00:22:48.339 --> 00:22:51.420
And I think this pushed me to do this case study.

00:22:51.420 --> 00:22:54.819
So when you have the chunk transfer encoding,

00:22:54.819 --> 00:22:58.020
which is this, you can also have chunk extensions.

00:22:58.020 --> 00:23:01.420
So after the column, you can have some parameters here

00:23:02.339 --> 00:23:07.339
that obviously probably no one in the whole world ever uses,

00:23:07.619 --> 00:23:10.300
but that's exactly what we as security professionals,

00:23:10.300 --> 00:23:13.260
we want to have a feature that nobody knows about,

00:23:13.260 --> 00:23:18.260
but HTTP libraries and servers have to support.

00:23:18.260 --> 00:23:22.780
So here there was also some case

00:23:22.780 --> 00:23:27.020
where a new line character in the chunk

00:23:27.020 --> 00:23:29.939
shouldn't be accepted, but can be accepted.

00:23:30.020 --> 00:23:33.300
And it led to do some bugs.

00:23:33.300 --> 00:23:36.500
Also a fun one, also a good, great area for research,

00:23:37.579 --> 00:23:41.699
because again, this feature is not used a lot.

00:23:41.699 --> 00:23:43.260
So maybe this is something

00:23:43.260 --> 00:23:45.459
that not all the servers get right.

00:23:45.459 --> 00:23:48.819
So if you're looking for areas for further research,

00:23:48.819 --> 00:23:52.099
I would definitely look at trailer parsing

00:23:52.099 --> 00:23:54.300
and chunk extensions.

00:23:54.300 --> 00:23:57.420
They look like very good candidates for bugs to me.

00:23:58.180 --> 00:24:00.339
That's it for this video.

00:24:00.339 --> 00:24:02.060
Remember that the full case study

00:24:02.060 --> 00:24:04.380
is in the BBRE Premium Archive,

00:24:04.380 --> 00:24:07.300
but if you want to watch another free case study video,

00:24:07.300 --> 00:24:08.579
check out this playlist

00:24:08.579 --> 00:24:11.619
because I've already published nine of them.

00:24:11.619 --> 00:24:14.500
For now, thank you for watching and goodbye.

